#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bin/ubx-users -- the users primitive's convergence PLANNER, a thin
EXECUTOR, and a real password-hash APPLIER (SPEC.md §4.3 "Users" row, §6,
§7, §8.1, §11 M4; GitHub issue #28, milestone M2; GitHub issue #80,
milestone M4, "password login from a secret-sourced hash").

-- Why this lives outside Nix, as a script under bin/ -----------------------

Exactly the same reasoning nix/archive.nix / bin/ubx-resolve split fetching
(pure, hash-pinned) from resolution (inherently impure -- "what does this
resolve to *right now*"): convergence is inherently a function of REAL,
observed system state (the actual /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/shadow on a
running machine) that Nix's build graph has no business seeing. nix/users.nix
owns the declaration surface and renders it to a flat JSON manifest (see that
file's header for the schema); this script CONSUMES that manifest plus
fixture (in tests) or real (at activation) copies of passwd/group/shadow and
decides what needs to change. Kept in one deterministic, pure-Python
file specifically so the planner half can be unit-tested with zero root,
network, or `nix` binary (tests/README.md's own rule) -- see
tests/unit/100-ubx-users-parsing.sh through 105-ubx-users-cli.sh.

-- The three subcommands, and why they're separate -------------------------

  ubx-users plan --manifest FILE --passwd FILE --group FILE --shadow FILE
                  [--exceptions FILE] [--home-state FILE]
                  [--secrets-manifest FILE] [--out FILE]
    Pure function of its inputs: declared manifest + observed passwd/group/
    shadow (+ optional exceptions/home-state/secrets-manifest fixtures) ->
    a deterministic JSON PLAN on stdout (or --out). Never touches a real
    system, and never reads real secret material -- see "Password hashes
    (hashedPasswordSecret, issue #80)" below. This is the half tests/unit/
    exercises heavily -- see "The plan schema" below.

  ubx-users execute --plan FILE [--format shell|json] [--out FILE]
    Translates an already-computed plan into the EXACT command sequence
    that converges a real system to it (useradd/usermod/groupadd/gpasswd,
    plus the authorized_keys file-materialization steps) -- emission only.
    Deliberately does NOT run anything itself: SPEC.md §11 M2 scoped this
    to "converge passwd/groups state" being *testable*, the same way
    issue #31's apt/dpkg/snap guards were unit-tested standalone with real
    activation-image wiring explicitly deferred (see docs/guards.md's "What
    is deferred"). Real execution of THIS subcommand's own output is
    follow-up work once that wiring exists; see docs/users.md. Password
    hashes are deliberately NOT part of this subcommand's own output --
    see immediately below.

  ubx-users apply-passwords --plan FILE --shadow FILE
                             [--run-secrets-dir DIR] [--apply | --dry-run]
                             [--out FILE]
    The one REAL executor in this file (unlike `execute` above): reads
    each `password_set` plan entry's referenced secret straight off its
    real, already-materialized runtime path (`--run-secrets-dir/<secret>`,
    i.e. `secrets.<name>.path`/`/run/secrets/<name>`, GitHub issue #78) and
    writes the resulting hash into `--shadow`'s matching user line, IN
    PLACE -- see "Password hashes (hashedPasswordSecret, issue #80)"
    below for the full design and why this is the one place in this file
    that both reads real secret material and mutates a real file. Exactly
    bin/ubx-secrets-apply's own "root only for real system files, no root
    needed against a temp fixture" posture: writing a plain file needs no
    special privilege by itself. `--dry-run` (the default) computes and
    reports the same changed/errors without writing anything.

Keeping `execute`/`apply-passwords` thin, separate translation/application
steps (rather than folding either into `plan`) is what SPEC.md's own
convergence-diagram row means by "Live switch ... users/home ... none
[downtime]": the plan is inspectable and diffable on its own before
anything is applied, and unit tests cover the planner's decisions in
complete isolation from how those decisions eventually get executed.

-- The manifest schema (produced by nix/users.nix's `renderManifestJSON`) --

  {
    "version": 1,
    "users": [
      { "name": ..., "uid": <int|null>, "system": <bool>,
        "shell": "/usr/bin/bash", "home": <str|null>, "createHome": <bool>,
        "groups": [ ... ], "authorizedKeys": [ "ssh-ed25519 ... ", ... ],
        "hashedPasswordSecret": <str|null> },
      ...
    ],
    "groups": [
      { "name": ..., "gid": <int|null>, "system": <bool> },
      ...
    ]
  }
`home: null` means "default to /home/<name>" (HOME_DEFAULT_FMT below);
`uid`/`gid: null` means "the planner auto-allocates one" (see "UID/GID
allocation" below). This exact schema is also accepted directly as a hand-
written JSON fixture by every planner test in tests/unit/ -- nothing about
`plan` requires having gone through Nix at all, matching this repo's "no
`nix` binary in the dev harness" constraint.

-- UID/GID allocation -------------------------------------------------------

Mirrors Debian/Ubuntu shadow-utils' own /etc/login.defs defaults (unchanged
on stock Ubuntu 24.04): SYS_UID_MIN/MAX = 100/999, UID_MIN/MAX = 1000/59999,
and the identical SYS_GID_MIN/MAX, GID_MIN/MAX for groups. A user/group's own
`system` flag selects the range; within it, the LOWEST free id (not already
used by an observed account, nor already handed out earlier in this same
planning run) is picked -- deterministic for a fixed observed-state input.
An explicit `uid`/`gid` is always honored verbatim (never range-checked
against `system`) -- see userType's own description in nix/users.nix for why.

-- What counts as "managed" -------------------------------------------------

The planner only ever looks at: (1) declared users/groups, and (2)
"required" groups -- every declared group, plus every group any declared
user's `groups` list references. It never inspects, reports on, or plans a
change to any OTHER passwd/group entry (SPEC.md §7's "never plan changes to
non-declared users or system accounts not under management"). Anomalies
found strictly WITHIN that managed domain (a managed group's observed
membership includes a user this manifest doesn't declare; an existing
managed group's observed gid disagrees with an explicit declared one; a
malformed passwd/group line) are reported as `drift` entries -- surfaced,
never silently auto-corrected (SPEC.md §7's converge-report posture) --
and do not by themselves fail the run (only `errors` -- uid/gid conflicts
with a FOREIGN account, or a bad `hashedPasswordSecret` reference -- do
that; see "Errors vs. drift" below).

-- Errors vs. drift ---------------------------------------------------------

A hard `errors` entry (nonzero exit) is reserved for: (1) a declared,
explicit uid/gid that a FOREIGN (non-declared) account already holds --
SPEC.md's own acceptance line for issue #28 is explicit: "declared uid
already taken by a foreign user -> error, not silent adoption" -- creating
(or renaming into) that identity would either fail at the OS level anyway
or, worse, silently start managing an account this manifest never
declared; and (2), as of issue #80, a declared `hashedPasswordSecret` that
cannot be validated against a real secrets manifest -- see "Password
hashes" below. Every other anomaly is `drift`: informational, inspectable,
and left for a human to resolve deliberately.

-- Machine-local exceptions -------------------------------------------------

`--exceptions FILE`: a JSON object `{ "<user>": ["shell", "home", "uid",
"groups", "authorizedKeys", "hashedPasswordSecret"], ... }` naming, per
declared user, which fields the planner must leave alone even if the
declared value disagrees with what it observes -- SPEC.md §4.2's
"machine-local mutable exceptions" concept, narrowed to this primitive's
own fields (no exceptions file at all, or a user/field pair not listed in
it, is fully converged as normal). `hashedPasswordSecret` in this list
skips PLANNING a `password_set` entry for that user (the reference is
still syntactically/referentially validated -- see "Password hashes"
below -- exceptions cover convergence intent, not manifest correctness).

-- authorizedKeys materialization -------------------------------------------

`--home-state FILE` (optional): a JSON object `{ "<user>": ["key line",
...], ... }` representing the authorized_keys lines *currently* observed for
that user (there is no such thing in passwd/group/shadow -- this is the
planner's one fixture input that isn't shadow-file-shaped). A declared
user's authorizedKeys plan entry is only emitted when it differs from this
(omitted user/key entirely -> treated as empty, i.e. "nothing observed
yet"); this is what makes the "no-op on an already-converged fixture"
acceptance case reachable even for a manifest that declares keys.

-- Password hashes (hashedPasswordSecret, issue #80) ------------------------

SPEC.md §6's own example: `hashedPasswordSecret = "gunnarPassword";`. This
is a REFERENCE -- the declared NAME of a secret in `secrets/index.nix`
(nix/secrets.nix's own declaration surface) -- never a hash or path value;
see that file's header, "hashedPasswordSecret: a reference, never a
value", for the structural reason the MANIFEST this script consumes can
carry that name completely safely. This script never reads real secret
material during `plan` -- only during `apply-passwords`, and even there
only the referenced secret's own runtime-materialized bytes
(`--run-secrets-dir/<name>`), never anything from the manifest itself.

  `plan`'s own validation (--secrets-manifest FILE, OPTIONAL as a flag but
  NOT optional in effect): for every declared user with a non-null
  `hashedPasswordSecret`, the referenced name must appear in
  `--secrets-manifest`'s own declared `secrets[].name` list (SPEC.md
  §8.1's manifest shape, nix/secrets.nix's `renderManifestJSON`) or `plan`
  reports a hard `errors` entry -- including when `--secrets-manifest`
  was not passed AT ALL (a `hashedPasswordSecret` with nothing to validate
  it against is refused, not silently trusted): "a missing/unreferenced
  secret is a clear, explicit error", this issue's own acceptance
  criterion. A validated reference (not excepted -- see "Machine-local
  exceptions" above) becomes one `password_set` entry:
  `{"user": ..., "secret": ...}` -- metadata only, still no hash. `plan`
  cannot diff this against the real, currently-set hash (it has no access
  to the real secret's bytes, nor to real /etc/shadow hash fields beyond
  "does this user exist" -- see `parse_shadow_names`) -- idempotency (skip
  a no-op rewrite) is therefore `apply-passwords`' own job, at the point
  it actually CAN compare hashes for real.

  `apply-passwords` (the real executor): for each `password_set` entry,
  reads `--run-secrets-dir/<secret>` (the real materialized secret file
  bin/ubx-secrets-apply produces, SPEC.md §8.1's `secrets.<name>.path`) --
  a missing file there is ALSO a clear, explicit error ("has the secrets
  domain been applied yet?"), never a silent skip -- and, if the resulting
  hash differs from that user's CURRENT shadow-file hash field, rewrites
  only that one field, preserving every other shadow field (lockout
  status, aging, etc.) verbatim. Already-matching hashes are left
  untouched: a converged user is a real no-op, not a spurious rewrite,
  exactly this project's standing idempotency posture for every other
  domain. `--dry-run` (the default) computes and reports the same
  changed/errors without ever writing `--shadow`.

  Ordering: `apply-passwords` depends on the secrets domain having ALREADY
  materialized `--run-secrets-dir/<secret>` for real -- bin/ubx's own
  `execute_domains` therefore runs the secrets domain's real applier
  (bin/ubx-secrets-apply) BEFORE calling this subcommand (SPEC.md §8.1;
  GitHub issue #80's own acceptance criterion, "secrets delivery must run
  before users convergence").
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import sys

PROG = "ubx-users"

# -- UID/GID range constants (Debian/Ubuntu shadow-utils defaults; see this
# file's own header, "UID/GID allocation") -----------------------------------
SYS_UID_MIN, SYS_UID_MAX = 100, 999
UID_MIN, UID_MAX = 1000, 59999
SYS_GID_MIN, SYS_GID_MAX = 100, 999
GID_MIN, GID_MAX = 1000, 59999

NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$")
KEY_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^\S+ \S+")

# Secret-name grammar for `hashedPasswordSecret` -- deliberately the exact
# same regex nix/secrets.nix's own `nameRe` (and nix/users.nix's own
# duplicate, `secretNameRe`) uses for a declared secret's attribute name:
# a plain Nix attribute identifier, NOT this file's own lowercase-only
# username `NAME_RE` above.
SECRET_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}$")

# A real /etc/shadow line's field count (name, hash, lastchange, min, max,
# warn, inactive, expire, reserved) -- shadow(5).
SHADOW_FIELD_COUNT = 9


def die(msg):
    print(f"{PROG}: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


# -- fixture parsing (passwd / group / shadow) -------------------------------
#
# Each parser returns (entries, malformed) rather than raising on a bad
# line: a single corrupt line in an otherwise-fine fixture/real file
# shouldn't abort the whole run -- it becomes a `malformed_*_line` drift
# entry instead (see this file's header, "What counts as managed"), and
# that one entry is simply excluded from further processing (never guessed
# at).


def parse_passwd(path):
    entries = {}
    malformed = []
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for lineno, raw in enumerate(f, start=1):
            line = raw.rstrip("\n")
            if line == "":
                continue
            fields = line.split(":")
            if len(fields) != 7 or not fields[2].isdigit() or not fields[3].isdigit():
                malformed.append({"file": "passwd", "line": lineno, "text": line})
                continue
            name, _passwd, uid, gid, gecos, home, shell = fields
            entries[name] = {
                "uid": int(uid),
                "gid": int(gid),
                "gecos": gecos,
                "home": home,
                "shell": shell,
            }
    return entries, malformed


def parse_group(path):
    entries = {}
    malformed = []
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for lineno, raw in enumerate(f, start=1):
            line = raw.rstrip("\n")
            if line == "":
                continue
            fields = line.split(":")
            if len(fields) != 4 or not fields[2].isdigit():
                malformed.append({"file": "group", "line": lineno, "text": line})
                continue
            name, _passwd, gid, members_field = fields
            members = [m for m in members_field.split(",") if m != ""]
            entries[name] = {"gid": int(gid), "members": members}
    return entries, malformed


def parse_shadow_names(path):
    names = set()
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for raw in f:
            line = raw.rstrip("\n")
            if line == "":
                continue
            name = line.split(":", 1)[0]
            if name:
                names.add(name)
    return names


def load_json(path, default):
    if path is None:
        return default
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        return json.load(f)


# -- manifest validation ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Defense-in-depth, independent of nix/users.nix's own eval-time checks
# (this script's tests hand-craft manifest.json fixtures directly, with no
# `nix` binary involved at all -- see this file's header): a manifest that
# reaches `plan` is re-validated here rather than trusted blindly.


def validate_manifest(manifest):
    errors = []
    if not isinstance(manifest, dict):
        return ["manifest top level is not a JSON object"]
    if manifest.get("version") != 1:
        errors.append(f"manifest 'version' must be 1, got {manifest.get('version')!r}")

    users = manifest.get("users")
    if not isinstance(users, list):
        errors.append("manifest 'users' must be a list")
        users = []
    groups = manifest.get("groups")
    if not isinstance(groups, list):
        errors.append("manifest 'groups' must be a list")
        groups = []

    seen_user_names = set()
    seen_uids = {}
    for i, u in enumerate(users):
        label = f"users[{i}]"
        if not isinstance(u, dict):
            errors.append(f"{label} is not an object")
            continue
        name = u.get("name")
        label = f"users[{i}] ({name!r})"
        if not isinstance(name, str) or not NAME_RE.match(name):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'name' is not a valid username (must match {NAME_RE.pattern})")
        elif name in seen_user_names:
            errors.append(f"duplicate declared user name: {name!r}")
        else:
            seen_user_names.add(name)

        uid = u.get("uid")
        if uid is not None and (not isinstance(uid, int) or isinstance(uid, bool) or uid < 0):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'uid' must be null or a non-negative integer, got {uid!r}")
        elif uid is not None:
            if uid in seen_uids:
                errors.append(
                    f"duplicate explicit uid {uid} declared by {seen_uids[uid]!r} and {name!r}"
                )
            else:
                seen_uids[uid] = name

        if not isinstance(u.get("system", False), bool):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'system' must be a boolean")
        if not isinstance(u.get("createHome", True), bool):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'createHome' must be a boolean")

        shell = u.get("shell")
        if not isinstance(shell, str) or not shell.startswith("/"):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'shell' must be an absolute path string, got {shell!r}")

        home = u.get("home")
        if home is not None and (not isinstance(home, str) or not home.startswith("/")):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'home' must be null or an absolute path string, got {home!r}")

        ugroups = u.get("groups", [])
        if not isinstance(ugroups, list) or not all(
            isinstance(g, str) and NAME_RE.match(g) for g in ugroups
        ):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'groups' must be a list of valid group names")

        keys = u.get("authorizedKeys", [])
        if not isinstance(keys, list) or not all(
            isinstance(k, str) and KEY_LINE_RE.match(k) and "\n" not in k and "\t" not in k
            for k in keys
        ):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'authorizedKeys' must be a list of single key lines")

        hps = u.get("hashedPasswordSecret")
        if hps is not None and (not isinstance(hps, str) or not SECRET_NAME_RE.match(hps)):
            errors.append(
                f"{label} 'hashedPasswordSecret' must be null or a valid secret name "
                f"(must match {SECRET_NAME_RE.pattern}), got {hps!r}"
            )

    seen_group_names = set()
    seen_gids = {}
    for i, g in enumerate(groups):
        label = f"groups[{i}]"
        if not isinstance(g, dict):
            errors.append(f"{label} is not an object")
            continue
        name = g.get("name")
        label = f"groups[{i}] ({name!r})"
        if not isinstance(name, str) or not NAME_RE.match(name):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'name' is not a valid group name (must match {NAME_RE.pattern})")
        elif name in seen_group_names:
            errors.append(f"duplicate declared group name: {name!r}")
        else:
            seen_group_names.add(name)

        gid = g.get("gid")
        if gid is not None and (not isinstance(gid, int) or isinstance(gid, bool) or gid < 0):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'gid' must be null or a non-negative integer, got {gid!r}")
        elif gid is not None:
            if gid in seen_gids:
                errors.append(
                    f"duplicate explicit gid {gid} declared by {seen_gids[gid]!r} and {name!r}"
                )
            else:
                seen_gids[gid] = name

        if not isinstance(g.get("system", False), bool):
            errors.append(f"{label} 'system' must be a boolean")

    return errors


def validate_secrets_manifest(doc):
    """A minimal shape check for a --secrets-manifest fixture (nix/secrets.nix's
    own renderManifestJSON output, SPEC.md §8.1) -- just enough to safely pull
    out the declared secret NAMES this file's own `plan` cross-checks
    `hashedPasswordSecret` against. Not a full re-validation of every secret
    field (that is bin/ubx-secrets' own `validate_manifest` job, a different
    script, a different manifest consumer)."""
    if not isinstance(doc, dict) or not isinstance(doc.get("secrets"), list):
        return None
    names = set()
    for s in doc["secrets"]:
        if isinstance(s, dict) and isinstance(s.get("name"), str):
            names.add(s["name"])
    return names


# -- id allocation ------------------------------------------------------------


def alloc_id(used, planned, is_system, kind):
    ranges = {
        ("user", False): (UID_MIN, UID_MAX),
        ("user", True): (SYS_UID_MIN, SYS_UID_MAX),
        ("group", False): (GID_MIN, GID_MAX),
        ("group", True): (SYS_GID_MIN, SYS_GID_MAX),
    }
    lo, hi = ranges[(kind, bool(is_system))]
    for candidate in range(lo, hi + 1):
        if candidate not in used and candidate not in planned:
            return candidate
    raise PlannerError(f"no free {'system' if is_system else 'normal'} {kind} id in range {lo}-{hi}")


class PlannerError(Exception):
    pass


# -- the planner --------------------------------------------------------------


def build_plan(manifest, passwd, group_entries, shadow_names, exceptions, home_state,
                passwd_malformed, group_malformed, secrets_manifest_names=None):
    errors = []
    drift = []

    declared_users = {u["name"]: u for u in manifest["users"]}
    declared_groups = {g["name"]: g for g in manifest["groups"]}

    for m in passwd_malformed:
        drift.append({"kind": "malformed_passwd_line", "line": m["line"], "text": m["text"]})
    for m in group_malformed:
        drift.append({"kind": "malformed_group_line", "line": m["line"], "text": m["text"]})

    # -- required groups: every declared group, plus every group any
    # declared user's `groups` list references (this file's header, "What
    # counts as managed").
    required_group_names = set(declared_groups) | {
        g for u in declared_users.values() for g in u.get("groups", [])
    }

    used_gids = {ent["gid"] for ent in group_entries.values()}
    planned_gids = set()
    groups_create = []
    for name in sorted(required_group_names):
        if name in group_entries:
            obs = group_entries[name]
            decl = declared_groups.get(name)
            if decl is not None and decl.get("gid") is not None and decl["gid"] != obs["gid"]:
                drift.append({
                    "kind": "group_gid_mismatch",
                    "group": name,
                    "declared_gid": decl["gid"],
                    "observed_gid": obs["gid"],
                })
            continue
        decl = declared_groups.get(name)
        gid = decl["gid"] if decl is not None else None
        system = bool(decl["system"]) if decl is not None else False
        if gid is not None:
            if gid in used_gids:
                errors.append(
                    f"group '{name}': declared gid {gid} is already used by a different "
                    "group in the observed /etc/group (not silently adopting)"
                )
                continue
        else:
            try:
                gid = alloc_id(used_gids | planned_gids, planned_gids, system, "group")
            except PlannerError as e:
                errors.append(str(e))
                continue
        planned_gids.add(gid)
        groups_create.append({"name": name, "gid": gid, "system": system})

    used_uids = {ent["uid"] for ent in passwd.values()}
    planned_uids = set()
    users_create = []
    users_modify = []
    membership_add = []
    membership_remove = []

    for name in sorted(declared_users):
        decl = declared_users[name]
        exc = set(exceptions.get(name, []))
        decl_home = decl.get("home") or f"/home/{name}"
        decl_groups = sorted(decl.get("groups", []))

        if name not in passwd:
            uid = decl.get("uid")
            if uid is not None:
                if uid in used_uids:
                    foreign = next(n for n, u in passwd.items() if u["uid"] == uid)
                    errors.append(
                        f"user '{name}': declared uid {uid} is already used by observed "
                        f"user '{foreign}' (not silently adopting)"
                    )
                    continue
            else:
                try:
                    uid = alloc_id(used_uids | planned_uids, planned_uids, bool(decl.get("system", False)), "user")
                except PlannerError as e:
                    errors.append(str(e))
                    continue
            planned_uids.add(uid)
            users_create.append({
                "name": name,
                "uid": uid,
                "system": bool(decl.get("system", False)),
                "shell": decl["shell"],
                "home": decl_home,
                "createHome": bool(decl.get("createHome", True)),
                "groups": decl_groups,
            })
            continue

        obs = passwd[name]
        changes = {}
        if "uid" not in exc and decl.get("uid") is not None and decl["uid"] != obs["uid"]:
            target = decl["uid"]
            foreign = next(
                (n for n, u in passwd.items() if u["uid"] == target and n != name), None
            )
            if foreign is not None:
                errors.append(
                    f"user '{name}': declared uid {target} is already used by observed "
                    f"user '{foreign}' (not silently adopting)"
                )
            else:
                changes["uid"] = {"from": obs["uid"], "to": target}
        if "shell" not in exc and decl["shell"] != obs["shell"]:
            changes["shell"] = {"from": obs["shell"], "to": decl["shell"]}
        if "home" not in exc and decl_home != obs["home"]:
            changes["home"] = {"from": obs["home"], "to": decl_home}
        if changes:
            users_modify.append({"name": name, "changes": changes})

        if name not in shadow_names:
            drift.append({"kind": "missing_shadow_entry", "user": name})

        if "groups" not in exc:
            observed_membership = {
                g for g, ent in group_entries.items() if name in ent["members"]
            }
            declared_membership = set(decl_groups)
            for g in sorted(declared_membership - observed_membership):
                membership_add.append({"user": name, "group": g})
            for g in sorted(observed_membership - declared_membership):
                membership_remove.append({"user": name, "group": g})

    # -- undeclared members of a managed (required) group: drift, never an
    # auto-planned removal (this file's header, "What counts as managed").
    for name in sorted(required_group_names):
        ent = group_entries.get(name)
        if ent is None:
            continue
        for member in sorted(ent["members"]):
            if member not in declared_users:
                drift.append({"kind": "undeclared_group_member", "group": name, "user": member})

    authorized_keys = []
    for name in sorted(declared_users):
        decl = declared_users[name]
        exc = set(exceptions.get(name, []))
        keys = decl.get("authorizedKeys", [])
        if not keys or "authorizedKeys" in exc:
            continue
        observed = home_state.get(name, [])
        if list(keys) == list(observed):
            continue
        home = decl.get("home") or f"/home/{name}"
        authorized_keys.append({
            "user": name,
            "dir": f"{home}/.ssh",
            "path": f"{home}/.ssh/authorized_keys",
            "dir_mode": "0700",
            "file_mode": "0600",
            "keys": list(keys),
        })

    # -- password_set (hashedPasswordSecret, GitHub issue #80) --------------
    #
    # See this file's header, "Password hashes (hashedPasswordSecret, issue
    # #80)", for the full design. Metadata only -- {"user", "secret"} -- the
    # real hash is never read here; a bad/unreferenced secret name is a hard
    # `errors` entry, not drift (this file's header, "Errors vs. drift").
    password_set = []
    for name in sorted(declared_users):
        decl = declared_users[name]
        exc = set(exceptions.get(name, []))
        secret = decl.get("hashedPasswordSecret")
        if secret is None:
            continue
        if secrets_manifest_names is None:
            errors.append(
                f"user '{name}': hashedPasswordSecret '{secret}' declared but no "
                "--secrets-manifest was given to validate it against (refusing to plan "
                "an unvalidated password source)"
            )
            continue
        if secret not in secrets_manifest_names:
            errors.append(
                f"user '{name}': hashedPasswordSecret '{secret}' is not declared in the "
                "secrets manifest (--secrets-manifest) -- no such secret"
            )
            continue
        if "hashedPasswordSecret" in exc:
            continue
        password_set.append({"user": name, "secret": secret})

    drift.sort(key=lambda d: json.dumps(d, sort_keys=True))
    errors.sort()

    empty = not (
        users_create or users_modify or groups_create
        or membership_add or membership_remove or authorized_keys or password_set
    )

    return {
        "version": 1,
        "empty": empty,
        "users": {
            "create": users_create,
            "modify": sorted(users_modify, key=lambda u: u["name"]),
        },
        "groups": {
            "create": sorted(groups_create, key=lambda g: g["name"]),
        },
        "membership": {
            "add": sorted(membership_add, key=lambda m: (m["group"], m["user"])),
            "remove": sorted(membership_remove, key=lambda m: (m["group"], m["user"])),
        },
        "authorized_keys": sorted(authorized_keys, key=lambda a: a["user"]),
        "password_set": sorted(password_set, key=lambda p: p["user"]),
        "drift": drift,
        "errors": errors,
    }


def write_json(obj, out_path):
    text = json.dumps(obj, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"
    if out_path is None:
        sys.stdout.write(text)
        return
    tmp = out_path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(text)
    os.replace(tmp, out_path)


def cmd_plan(args):
    manifest = load_json(args.manifest, None)
    if manifest is None:
        die("--manifest is required")
    m_errors = validate_manifest(manifest)
    if m_errors:
        die("manifest failed validation:\n" + "\n".join(f"  - {e}" for e in m_errors))

    passwd, passwd_malformed = parse_passwd(args.passwd)
    group_entries, group_malformed = parse_group(args.group)
    shadow_names = parse_shadow_names(args.shadow)
    exceptions = load_json(args.exceptions, {})
    home_state = load_json(args.home_state, {})

    secrets_manifest_names = None
    if args.secrets_manifest is not None:
        secrets_doc = load_json(args.secrets_manifest, None)
        secrets_manifest_names = validate_secrets_manifest(secrets_doc)
        if secrets_manifest_names is None:
            die(
                f"--secrets-manifest {args.secrets_manifest!r} is not a valid secrets "
                "manifest (expected {\"version\": 1, \"secrets\": [...]})"
            )

    plan = build_plan(
        manifest, passwd, group_entries, shadow_names, exceptions, home_state,
        passwd_malformed, group_malformed, secrets_manifest_names,
    )
    write_json(plan, args.out)
    sys.exit(1 if plan["errors"] else 0)


# -- the executor: plan -> exact command sequence (emission only) -----------
#
# See this file's header, "The three subcommands, and why they're
# separate": this never runs anything itself. Ordering is fixed and
# deterministic: groups.create, users.create, users.modify,
# membership.remove, membership.add, authorized_keys -- each already
# sorted by the planner (groups/users by name, membership by (group,
# user)), so iterating them in plan order is itself deterministic.
# `password_set` is deliberately NOT translated into a step here -- see
# "Password hashes" in this file's header for why that domain has its own
# real executor (`apply-passwords`) instead of an emitted-only command.


def build_steps(plan):
    steps = []

    for g in plan["groups"]["create"]:
        argv = ["groupadd", "-g", str(g["gid"])]
        if g["system"]:
            argv.append("-r")
        argv.append(g["name"])
        steps.append({"op": "run", "argv": argv})

    for u in plan["users"]["create"]:
        argv = ["useradd", "-u", str(u["uid"])]
        if u["system"]:
            argv.append("-r")
        argv.append("-m" if u["createHome"] else "-M")
        argv += ["-d", u["home"], "-s", u["shell"]]
        if u["groups"]:
            argv += ["-G", ",".join(sorted(u["groups"]))]
        argv.append(u["name"])
        steps.append({"op": "run", "argv": argv})

    for u in plan["users"]["modify"]:
        changes = u["changes"]
        argv = ["usermod"]
        if "uid" in changes:
            argv += ["-u", str(changes["uid"]["to"])]
        if "home" in changes:
            argv += ["-d", changes["home"]["to"]]
        if "shell" in changes:
            argv += ["-s", changes["shell"]["to"]]
        argv.append(u["name"])
        steps.append({"op": "run", "argv": argv})

    for m in plan["membership"]["remove"]:
        steps.append({"op": "run", "argv": ["gpasswd", "-d", m["user"], m["group"]]})
    for m in plan["membership"]["add"]:
        steps.append({"op": "run", "argv": ["gpasswd", "-a", m["user"], m["group"]]})

    for a in plan["authorized_keys"]:
        content = "".join(line + "\n" for line in a["keys"])
        steps.append({"op": "mkdir", "path": a["dir"], "mode": a["dir_mode"]})
        steps.append({"op": "chown", "path": a["dir"], "owner": a["user"], "group": a["user"]})
        steps.append({"op": "write_file", "path": a["path"], "mode": a["file_mode"], "content": content})
        steps.append({"op": "chown", "path": a["path"], "owner": a["user"], "group": a["user"]})

    return steps


def shquote(s):
    if s == "" or re.search(r"[^A-Za-z0-9_./:,=-]", s):
        return "'" + s.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"
    return s


def steps_to_shell(steps):
    lines = ["#!/bin/sh", "set -eu", ""]
    heredoc_i = 0
    for step in steps:
        op = step["op"]
        if op == "run":
            lines.append(" ".join(shquote(a) for a in step["argv"]))
        elif op == "mkdir":
            lines.append(f"mkdir -p {shquote(step['path'])}")
            lines.append(f"chmod {step['mode']} {shquote(step['path'])}")
        elif op == "chown":
            lines.append(f"chown {shquote(step['owner'])}:{shquote(step['group'])} {shquote(step['path'])}")
        elif op == "write_file":
            heredoc_i += 1
            delim = f"UBX_USERS_WRITE_FILE_{heredoc_i}"
            lines.append(f"cat > {shquote(step['path'])} <<'{delim}'")
            lines.append(step["content"].rstrip("\n"))
            lines.append(delim)
            lines.append(f"chmod {step['mode']} {shquote(step['path'])}")
        else:  # pragma: no cover -- defensive; build_steps never emits this
            raise PlannerError(f"unknown step op: {op!r}")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def cmd_execute(args):
    plan = load_json(args.plan, None)
    if plan is None:
        die("--plan is required")
    steps = build_steps(plan)
    if args.format == "json":
        write_json(steps, args.out)
    else:
        text = steps_to_shell(steps)
        if args.out is None:
            sys.stdout.write(text)
        else:
            tmp = args.out + ".tmp"
            with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(text)
            os.replace(tmp, args.out)
    sys.exit(0)


# -- apply-passwords: the real password-hash executor (issue #80) -----------
#
# See this file's header, "Password hashes (hashedPasswordSecret, issue
# #80)", for the full design. Unlike `execute` above, this SUBCOMMAND
# performs real I/O: it reads a real (or, in tests, fixture) secret file
# and, unless --dry-run, really rewrites --shadow's matching line.


def read_shadow_lines(path):
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return []
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines = lines[:-1]
    return lines


def apply_passwords(plan, run_secrets_dir, shadow_path, dry_run=False):
    """Applies plan['password_set'] (see build_plan's own comment for its
    shape) against `shadow_path`, sourcing each entry's real hash from
    `run_secrets_dir/<secret>` (the runtime secret path bin/ubx-secrets-apply
    materializes, SPEC.md §8.1's `secrets.<name>.path`). Returns
    `(changed, errors)`: `changed` is the sorted list of usernames whose
    shadow line's hash field actually differed and was (unless `dry_run`)
    rewritten -- an already-matching hash is left untouched, so a fully
    converged input is a real, idempotent no-op, matching every other
    domain's own apply posture. `errors` is a list of human-readable
    strings for anything that could not be resolved (a missing
    materialized secret, an empty secret, an unknown user, or a malformed
    shadow line) -- never raises for one of these expected, reportable
    conditions (mirrors build_plan's own `errors` list).

    The hash value itself NEVER appears in `errors`/`changed` (or, at any
    point in this file, in a manifest or plan) -- only user/secret NAMES
    and paths do; see this file's header, "THE ABSOLUTE INVARIANT" (via
    nix/secrets.nix's own, which this function's own posture mirrors).
    """
    entries = plan.get("password_set", [])
    errors = []
    if not entries:
        return [], errors

    lines = read_shadow_lines(shadow_path)
    index = {}
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if not line:
            continue
        index[line.split(":", 1)[0]] = i

    changed = []
    for entry in sorted(entries, key=lambda e: e["user"]):
        user = entry["user"]
        secret = entry["secret"]
        secret_path = os.path.join(run_secrets_dir, secret)
        if not os.path.isfile(secret_path):
            errors.append(
                f"user '{user}': hashedPasswordSecret '{secret}' has no materialized "
                f"secret at {secret_path} (has the secrets domain been applied yet? "
                "see this file's header, 'Password hashes')"
            )
            continue
        with open(secret_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            new_hash = f.read().strip()
        if not new_hash:
            errors.append(f"user '{user}': secret '{secret}' at {secret_path} is empty")
            continue
        if user not in index:
            errors.append(
                f"user '{user}': not present in {shadow_path} (create the user, e.g. "
                "via 'ubx-users execute', before setting its password)"
            )
            continue
        fields = lines[index[user]].split(":")
        if len(fields) != SHADOW_FIELD_COUNT:
            errors.append(
                f"user '{user}': malformed shadow line in {shadow_path} (expected "
                f"{SHADOW_FIELD_COUNT} colon-separated fields, got {len(fields)})"
            )
            continue
        if fields[1] == new_hash:
            continue
        fields[1] = new_hash
        lines[index[user]] = ":".join(fields)
        changed.append(user)

    if changed and not dry_run:
        tmp = shadow_path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("".join(line + "\n" for line in lines))
        os.replace(tmp, shadow_path)

    return sorted(changed), errors


def cmd_apply_passwords(args):
    plan = load_json(args.plan, None)
    if plan is None:
        die("--plan is required")
    changed, errors = apply_passwords(plan, args.run_secrets_dir, args.shadow, dry_run=not args.apply)
    write_json({"applied": bool(args.apply), "changed": changed, "errors": errors}, args.out)
    sys.exit(1 if errors else 0)


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description=(
            "Users-primitive convergence planner, thin command-sequence "
            "executor, and password-hash applier (SPEC.md §4.3 'Users'; "
            "issue #28, milestone M2; issue #80, milestone M4)."
        ),
    )
    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    p_plan = sub.add_parser("plan", help="compute a convergence plan from a manifest + observed state")
    p_plan.add_argument("--manifest", required=True, help="declared users/groups manifest (nix/users.nix's renderManifestJSON output)")
    p_plan.add_argument("--passwd", required=True, help="observed /etc/passwd (or a fixture copy)")
    p_plan.add_argument("--group", required=True, help="observed /etc/group (or a fixture copy)")
    p_plan.add_argument("--shadow", required=True, help="observed /etc/shadow (or a fixture copy)")
    p_plan.add_argument("--exceptions", default=None, help="optional machine-local-exceptions JSON")
    p_plan.add_argument("--home-state", default=None, dest="home_state", help="optional observed authorized_keys JSON")
    p_plan.add_argument("--secrets-manifest", default=None, dest="secrets_manifest",
                         help="declared secrets manifest (nix/secrets.nix's renderManifestJSON output) -- "
                              "cross-checks every declared hashedPasswordSecret against it")
    p_plan.add_argument("--out", default=None, help="write the plan here instead of stdout")
    p_plan.set_defaults(func=cmd_plan)

    p_exec = sub.add_parser("execute", help="translate a plan into the exact command sequence (emission only)")
    p_exec.add_argument("--plan", required=True, help="a plan produced by 'plan'")
    p_exec.add_argument("--format", choices=["shell", "json"], default="shell", help="output format (default: shell)")
    p_exec.add_argument("--out", default=None, help="write the sequence here instead of stdout")
    p_exec.set_defaults(func=cmd_execute)

    p_apply_pw = sub.add_parser(
        "apply-passwords",
        help="set /etc/shadow password hashes for hashedPasswordSecret users from their real, materialized secrets",
    )
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--plan", required=True, help="a plan produced by 'plan' (reads its password_set entries)")
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--shadow", required=True, help="the shadow file to update (real /etc/shadow, or a fixture copy)")
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--run-secrets-dir", default="/run/secrets", dest="run_secrets_dir",
                             help="the real (or, for tests, temp) canonical root each password_set entry's secret is read from, as DIR/<secret> (default /run/secrets)")
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--apply", dest="apply", action="store_true", default=False, help="actually rewrite --shadow (default: dry-run)")
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--dry-run", dest="apply", action="store_false", help="compute and report only, never write --shadow (default)")
    p_apply_pw.add_argument("--out", default=None, help="write the changed/errors summary here instead of stdout")
    p_apply_pw.set_defaults(func=cmd_apply_passwords)

    args = parser.parse_args()
    try:
        args.func(args)
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
        die(str(e))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
